<div class="exam-page">

    <div class="student-info">
        <h2 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold;">Mendoza, Stephanie P.  BSIT32E1</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="exam-content">

        <div class="welcome-text">
            <h2>IT ELECTIVE 3 - PRELIM A1 </h2>
            <p>Questions and answers for IT Elective 3</p>
        </div>

        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-number">
                QUESTION 01
            </div>

            <h3>
                What is the fundamental difference between Server-Side Rendering (SSR/MVC) and a Decoupled Web API architecture?
            </h3>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">A</span>
                    <span>SSR returns JSON payloads, whereas a Web API compiles .cshtml Razor views on the server.</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span class="letter">B</span>
                    <span>
                        SSR compiles HTML views on the server and sends fully rendered markup to the browser, whereas a Web API acts as a stateless data provider returning raw JSON.
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">C</span>
                    <span>
                        SSR only works with mobile applications, whereas Web APIs are restricted to desktop web browsers.
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">D</span>
                    <span>SSR requires client-side routing in React, whereas Web APIs manage HTML layouts directly on the server.</span>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>ANSWER</strong>
                <span>B. SSR compiles HTML views on the server and sends fully rendered markup to the browser, whereas a Web API acts as a stateless data provider returning raw JSON.</span>
            </div>

        </div>

        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-number">
                QUESTION 02
            </div>

            <h3>
                Why do Web API controllers in ASP.NET Core inherit from ControllerBase instead of Controller?
            </h3>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">A</span>
                    <span>ControllerBase supports Razor View rendering using View() and ViewData</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span class="letter">B</span>
                    <span>ControllerBase provides essential HTTP handling and routing without carrying unnecessary HTML view rendering overhead</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">C</span>
                    <span>Controller is obsolete in .NET 8 and cannot handle HTTP POST requests</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">D</span>
                    <span>ControllerBase automatically creates SQLite database files on application startup</span>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>ANSWER</strong>
                <span>B. ControllerBase provides essential HTTP handling and routing without carrying unnecessary HTML view rendering overhead</span>
            </div>

        </div>

        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-number">
                QUESTION 03
            </div>

            <h3>
                Which framework attribute automatically triggers an HTTP 400 Bad Request response when incoming request payloads fail structural model validation?
            </h3>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">A</span>
                    <span>[Route]</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">B</span>
                    <span>[HttpGet]</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span class="letter">C</span>
                    <span>[ApiController]</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">D</span>
                    <span>[FromQuery]</span>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>ANSWER</strong>
                <span>C. [ApiController]</span>
            </div>

        </div>

        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-number">
                QUESTION 04
            </div>

            <h3>
                Which HTTP status code should an API return after successfully creating a new resource via a POST request, along with a Location header pointing to the new URI?
            </h3>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">A</span>
                    <span>200 OK</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span class="letter">B</span>
                    <span>201 Created</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">C</span>
                    <span>204 No Content</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">D</span>
                    <span>404 Not Found</span>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>ANSWER</strong>
                <span>B. 201 Created</span>
            </div>

        </div>

        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-number">
                QUESTION 05
            </div>

            <h3>
                When a DELETE request successfully removes a resource from the server and returns no content in the response body, which HTTP status code is standard?
            </h3>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">A</span>
                    <span>200 OK</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">B</span>
                    <span>201 Created</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span class="letter">C</span>
                    <span>204 No Content</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">D</span>
                    <span>400 Bad Request</span>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>ANSWER</strong>
                <span>C. 204 No Content</span>
            </div>

        </div>

        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-number">
                QUESTION 06
            </div>

            <h3>
                Which of the following endpoint URIs follows RESTful resource-oriented naming best practices?
            </h3>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">A</span>
                    <span>GET /api/getAllCourses</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span class="letter">B</span>
                    <span>GET /api/courses</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">C</span>
                    <span>POST /api/createNewCourseRecord</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">D</span>
                    <span>POST /api/deleteCourseById?id=5</span>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>ANSWER</strong>
                <span>B. GET /api/courses</span>
            </div>

        </div>

        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-number">
                QUESTION 07
            </div>

            <h3>
                What is the primary function of the [Route("api/[controller]")] attribute in ASP.NET Core?
            </h3>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">A</span>
                    <span>It connects the controller directly to an Entity Framework database context</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span class="letter">B</span>
                    <span>It maps incoming URI request paths to the controller class using the class name token</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">C</span>
                    <span>It encrypts all incoming JSON payloads</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">D</span>
                    <span>It enables Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) for external React apps</span>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>ANSWER</strong>
                <span>B. It maps incoming URI request paths to the controller class using the class name token</span>
            </div>

        </div>

        <div class="question-card">

            <div class="question-number">
                QUESTION 08
            </div>

            <h3>
                Which HTTP verb is safe and idempotent, intended exclusively for retrieving resource data without altering server state?
            </h3>

            <div class="choices">

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">A</span>
                    <span>POST</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">B</span>
                    <span>PUT</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice correct">
                    <span class="letter">C</span>
                    <span>GET</span>
                </div>

                <div class="choice">
                    <span class="letter">D</span>
                    <span>DELETE</span>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="answer-box">
                <strong>ANSWER</strong>
                <span>C. GET</span>
            </div>

        </div>

